{"name":"Tropical Cyclone","hex":"#b9cabd","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tropical cyclone","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rapidly rotating storm system characterized by a low-pressure center, strong winds, and a circular or spiral arrangement of thunderstorms that produce heavy rain, ranging in increasing strength from tropical depressions and tropical storm to hurricanes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tropical_cyclone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tropical cyclone","blocks":[{"text":"A tropical cyclone is a rapidly rotating storm system with a low-pressure area, a closed low-level atmospheric circulation, strong winds, and a spiral arrangement of thunderstorms that produce heavy rain and squalls. Depending on its location and strength, a tropical cyclone is called a hurricane, typhoon, tropical storm, cyclonic storm, tropical depression, or simply cyclone. A hurricane is a strong tropical cyclone that occurs in the Atlantic Ocean or northeastern Pacific Ocean. A typhoon is the same thing which occurs in the northwestern Pacific Ocean. In the Indian Ocean and South Pacific, comparable storms are referred to as \"tropical cyclones\". In modern times, on average around 80 to 90 named tropical cyclones form each year around the world, over half of which develop hurricane-force winds of 65 kn or more."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Dramatic_Views_of_Hurricane_Florence_from_the_International_Space_Station_From_9_12_%2842828603210%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Dramatic_Views_of_Hurricane_Florence_from_the_International_Space_Station_From_9_12_%2842828603210%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_cyclone","wikidata":"Q8092"},{"title":"Cyclone","blocks":[{"text":"In meteorology, a cyclone is a large air mass that rotates around a strong center of low atmospheric pressure, counterclockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and clockwise in the Southern Hemisphere as viewed from above. Cyclones are characterized by inward-spiraling winds that rotate about a zone of low pressure."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Low_pressure_system_over_Iceland.jpg/330px-Low_pressure_system_over_Iceland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyclone","wikidata":"Q79602"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Dramatic_Views_of_Hurricane_Florence_from_the_International_Space_Station_From_9_12_%2842828603210%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Dramatic_Views_of_Hurricane_Florence_from_the_International_Space_Station_From_9_12_%2842828603210%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Dramatic_Views_of_Hurricane_Florence_from_the_International_Space_Station_From_9_12_%2842828603210%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Dramatic_Views_of_Hurricane_Florence_from_the_International_Space_Station_From_9_12_%2842828603210%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Dramatic_Views_of_Hurricane_Florence_from_the_International_Space_Station_From_9_12_%2842828603210%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Dramatic_Views_of_Hurricane_Florence_from_the_International_Space_Station_From_9_12_%2842828603210%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hurricane Florence viewed from the International Space Station in 2018. The eye, eyewall, and surrounding rainbands are characteristics of tropical cyclones.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADramatic_Views_of_Hurricane_Florence_from_the_International_Space_Station_From_9_12_(42828603210)_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kate_2003-10-04_1420Z_%28alternate%29.jpg/500px-Kate_2003-10-04_1420Z_%28alternate%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kate_2003-10-04_1420Z_%28alternate%29.jpg/500px-Kate_2003-10-04_1420Z_%28alternate%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKate_2003-10-04_1420Z_(alternate).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Paulette_2020-09-10_1620Z.jpg/500px-Paulette_2020-09-10_1620Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Paulette_2020-09-10_1620Z.jpg/1280px-Paulette_2020-09-10_1620Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Paulette_2020-09-10_1620Z.jpg/500px-Paulette_2020-09-10_1620Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hurricane Paulette, in 2020, is an example of a sheared tropical cyclone, with deep convection slightly removed from the center of the system.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APaulette_2020-09-10_1620Z.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Maria%2C_Bopha_and_Saomai_2006-08-07_0435Z.jpg/500px-Maria%2C_Bopha_and_Saomai_2006-08-07_0435Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Maria%2C_Bopha_and_Saomai_2006-08-07_0435Z.jpg/1280px-Maria%2C_Bopha_and_Saomai_2006-08-07_0435Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Maria%2C_Bopha_and_Saomai_2006-08-07_0435Z.jpg/500px-Maria%2C_Bopha_and_Saomai_2006-08-07_0435Z.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Three tropical cyclones of the 2006 Pacific typhoon season at different stages of development. The weakest (left) demonstrates only the most basic circular shape. A stronger storm (top right) demonstrates spiral banding and increased centralization, while the strongest (lower right) has developed an eye.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaria%2C_Bopha_and_Saomai_2006-08-07_0435Z.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Staring_Down_Hurricane_Florence.jpg/500px-Staring_Down_Hurricane_Florence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Staring_Down_Hurricane_Florence.jpg/1280px-Staring_Down_Hurricane_Florence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Staring_Down_Hurricane_Florence.jpg/500px-Staring_Down_Hurricane_Florence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The eye and surrounding clouds of Hurricane Florence seen from the International Space Station","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStaring_Down_Hurricane_Florence.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Ioke_2006_track.png/500px-Ioke_2006_track.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Ioke_2006_track.png/1280px-Ioke_2006_track.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Ioke_2006_track.png/500px-Ioke_2006_track.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Storm track of Typhoon Ioke, showing recurvature off the Japanese coast in 2006","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIoke_2006_track.png"},{"srcset":"